.\" tmux-companion(1) -- the manual.
.\"
.\" mdoc, the macro set tmux's own page uses, so the two read alike when
.\" somebody has both open. Render it without installing:
.\"
.\"     man ./docs/tmux-companion.1
.\"
.\" Every subcommand here is checked against the binary by a test, so a
.\" command added without a paragraph in this file fails the build.
.Dd September 26, 2026
.Dt TMUX-COMPANION 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm tmux-companion
.Nd a status line, a set of pickers and a project manager for tmux
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Ar command
.Op Ar flags
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is one binary in two modes.
.Pp
.Ic server
binds a unix socket and answers requests forever, holding the caches that
make a status line cheap.
Every other command is a client: it connects, starting the server when the
socket is absent, writes one JSON line, reads one back, prints it and exits.
.Pp
The point of the split is the status bar.
tmux gates
.Ql #()
to
.Ev status-interval
per attached client, and a spawn costs 12.4 ms of CPU before the command has
computed anything, so the bill is the number of distinct commands rather than
what they do.
.Ic status-right
answers with git, bandwidth and battery in one call.
.Pp
Commands that draw on a terminal -- the pickers, the dialogs -- run in the
client process.
The daemon has no terminal; it answers with rows and the client draws them.
.Ss Daemon lifetime
One daemon answers for one socket.
A start takes an exclusive lock on
.Pa <socket>.lock
before it touches the socket file, and a start that cannot take the lock exits
quietly, leaving the daemon that holds it to answer.
Without the lock two starts in the same moment both unlink the socket file and
both bind, and the one whose file was taken away keeps running where no client
can reach it.
.Pp
A daemon exits when the socket file it bound is deleted or replaced, which it
checks every thirty seconds.
That is the only thing that ends a daemon besides a signal or
.Ic __shutdown ,
and it is what stops an abandoned daemon from outliving the socket it was
started for.
The next client starts a fresh one, which costs one cold
.Xr git 1
status.
.Pp
.Ic __shutdown
removes the socket file on its way out, so a path with no socket file means no
daemon rather than a daemon that has stopped answering.
.Sh GETTING IN
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic start Oo Ar directory Oc Op Fl -last , Fl -hook
The way in, from a shell that is not in tmux yet.
Opens the project picker, live sessions first and every directory the
configured source knows underneath, and attaches to what is chosen.
The source is
.Xr zoxide 1
unless
.Ic [project] dirs_source
says otherwise.
.Fl -last
goes back to the session used most recently without asking, and a directory
skips the picker.
Inside tmux it switches rather than attaching.
.Pp
.Ql tmux
on its own leaves you in a session called
.Ql 0
holding one bare shell, which is what this replaces.
.Fl -hook
is for the
.Ic client-attached
hook in
.Pa tmux.conf ,
so that plain
.Ql tmux
lands in the same picker: it opens only when the session is one tmux named
itself, all digits, with one window, one pane and a shell in it, and leaves
any other session alone.
.It Ic click Ar range
What a mouse click on a segment of the right side does.
The
.Ic agents
and
.Ic health
segments carry a
.Ql range=user|NAME
mark, the way tmux marks its own window list, so a
.Ql MouseDown1StatusRight
binding of
.Ql run-shell "tmux-companion click '#{mouse_status_range}'"
reaches this with the segment's name; it runs what
.Ic [[status.right.segments]] on_click
says for that segment, or the default: the inbox for the agent count, the
brief for the health mark, nothing for the rest.
.It Ic brief Op Fl -print , Fl -hook
What needs you, on one screen: the agents waiting and what each one asked,
the reasons the health mark is up, the sessions nobody has touched for three
days, how long a sitting has run when the chunk clock is on, quiet hours when
they are on, and one line of numbers with when the last snapshot was.
Everything on it is a command of its own; this is the one look on sitting
down, and somewhere to act from.
.Pp
In a terminal it waits for keys, and a footer names the ones that do
something right now:
.Bl -tag -width Ds -compact
.It Ar number
go to that waiting agent's pane, or to that idle session; the numbers run on
from the agents into the idle sessions, and with ten rows or more a digit
that could start a longer number waits for the next digit or enter
.It Ic c Ar number
close that idle session, asking first, its layout saved, as
.Ic sessions idle
does
.It Ic h
acknowledge the health reasons, as
.Ic health ack
does
.It Ic d
show the
.Ic doctor
report, and come back on enter
.It Ic i
open the
.Ic inbox
picker
.It Ic j
open today's
.Ic journal
picker
.It Ic s
save a snapshot, as
.Ic sessions save
does
.It Ic z
an hour of quiet, or with quiet on, the end of it
.It Ic q , Ic Esc , Ic Enter
close
.El
.Pp
Going somewhere, and
.Ic i
and
.Ic j ,
end the brief.
The rest say one line about what they did and draw the brief again.
.Fl -print
prints and exits, with no numbers and no keys.
.Fl -hook
is for the
.Ic client-attached
hook: it opens the popup only when something is waiting or wrong, and says
nothing when the server is quiet.
In the week after a build first runs, a line counts the
.Ic setup
items still open, when there are any.
.It Ic setup Op Fl -print
Lists what the tool offers and the state of each:
.Ql on ,
.Ql open ,
.Ql off-by-choice ,
.Ql skipped
or
.Ql cant-tell .
Enter copies the lines to the clipboard and asks before adding them to
.Pa tmux.conf
or
.Pa config.toml .
Ctrl-x skips a row.
Ctrl-e sets a binding's key.
.Fl -print
prints id, group, state, key and line, tab-separated.
.El
.Sh STATUS LINE COMMANDS
These print tmux format strings on standard output and are meant for
.Ql #()
in
.Pa tmux.conf .
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic status-right Oo Ar path Oc Op Fl -style Ar style , Fl -branch-max-len Ar n
Git, bandwidth and battery in one call, computed concurrently, and the agent
count and the health mark when
.Ic [status.right]
asks for them.
The health mark is one glyph and a word when a timer failed in the last hour
and hasn't worked since,
.Pa config.toml
changed after the daemon started, or the binary on disk is newer than the
daemon running; nothing when the daemon is fine.
With
.Ic [online] enabled
it also reads
.Ql offline
when two TCP connections in a row to
.Ic [online] probe
went unanswered,
.Ic [online] interval_secs
apart; it is off until turned on, since the probe reaches an address outside
the machine.
One
.Ql #()
rather than three.
The
.Ic agents
segment reads
.Ql 4 agents \(bu 1 waiting :
how many panes run one of
.Ic [agents] programs ,
and how many of those have drawn nothing for
.Ic [agents] waiting_secs .
.Ic [agents] style
set to
.Ql glyphs
draws the robot and the count, then an arrow, an hourglass and the waiting
count, with nothing spelled out.
.Ic [agents] show
picks the count after the total:
.Ql waiting ,
the agents that have stopped, whether a hook said
.Cm asked
or
.Cm done ,
the bell rang, or the window went quiet;
.Ql busy ,
the agents that are working, drawn with the progress glyph, for somebody who
keeps a dozen open and works one at a time; or
.Ql both .
It draws nothing when no agent is running, and the pane list behind it is read
at most once per
.Ic [agents] interval_secs
however many clients are attached, and never when the segment is not
configured.
Takes a path and deliberately no pane pid: a pid makes the git segment
enumerate every process on the machine, which cost 18.5 ms of its 26.0.
.Fl -style
is one of
.Ql fill ,
.Ql outline
or
.Ql outline-bright ,
and anything else is refused with that list.
.Fl -branch-max-len
middle-ellipsizes a branch name longer than
.Ar n ;
without the flag
.Ic [git] branch_max_len
decides, 20 out of the box.
.It Ic gst Oo Ar path Oc Oo Ar pid Oc Op Fl -no-daemon Fl -no-tmux
The git segment on its own: branch, what is dirty, ahead and behind, with the
same
.Fl -style
and
.Fl -branch-max-len .
With a pid it also marks a suspended editor.
A daemon that answers with an error exits this command non-zero, which tmux
ignores in a
.Ql #()
and a prompt or a script can read.
.It Ic battery
Charge, as an icon and a percentage.
Reads the platform's battery and prints nothing on a machine without one.
.It Ic net Op Fl -no-daemon Fl -no-tmux
Bytes per second in and out, over all interfaces but the loopback.
A rate, so it is never cached.
Draws nothing below
.Ic [network] threshold_bps .
.It Ic sh-jobs Ar pane-pid
What is stopped or running under a pane, from the table in
.Ic [[sh_jobs.job]] .
.It Ic clients Ar attached Ar active
A marker when more than one client is looking at this session.
.It Ic window Fl i Ar index Op Ar flags
One window's entry for
.Ev window-status-format .
Costs a process spawn per window per redraw, and tmux redraws on pane output
as well as on the timer, so this is the expensive way to draw a window list.
.It Ic preview
Sample git segments in every colour style, without a server.
.El
.Ss Outside tmux
.Ic gst
and
.Ic net
are ordinary commands that print a string, and neither needs tmux to be
running.
Two flags make them usable anywhere a shell prompt, a bar or a script wants
them.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl -no-tmux
Write ANSI escapes instead of tmux's
.Ql #[fg=...]
markup, and reset the terminal at the end so nothing after the segment
inherits its colour.
A segment that drew nothing prints nothing, not even a newline.
.It Fl -no-daemon
Compute the answer in this process and exit.
No socket is opened, no server is started and nothing is left running.
The cost is the cache: every call pays for a cold
.Xr git 1
status, about 51 ms against a large tree, where the daemon answers a warm one
in well under a millisecond.
That is the right trade for a shell prompt and the wrong one for a status bar
refreshing once a second.
.Pp
.Ic net
is a rate and needs two counter readings, so without a daemon to hold the
first one it is kept in
.Pa $XDG_STATE_HOME/tmux-companion/net-sample .
The first call after a reboot records the reading and draws nothing, which is
what the daemon does on its first call and for the same reason.
.El
.Pp
For a prompt, in
.Xr bash 1
or
.Xr zsh 1 :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
PS1='$(tmux-companion gst --no-daemon --no-tmux) $ '
.Ed
.Pp
Inside tmux, leave both flags off: the daemon is what makes the segment cost
almost nothing to draw once a second.
.Sh PICKERS
Each is a fuzzy-matched list with a preview.
They are bound to keys in
.Pa docs/tmux.conf.full.example ;
the bindings shown are that file's.
.Pp
Every picker takes the same keys.
Typing filters, the arrows and Page Up and Page Down move, enter takes the
row, and escape, ctrl-c or ctrl-d closes it.
Ctrl-a, ctrl-u or Delete clears the filter, ctrl-p or Tab steps the preview
through its sizes, so the list beside it widens and narrows and the last step
hides the preview, ctrl-j and ctrl-k scroll it, and alt-enter takes what was
typed rather than the row.
A picker with a preview says Tab and ctrl-j/k in its line of keys, unless the
line is your own from
.Ic hint
in the config.
The terminal's cursor sits on the selected row, which is what a screen reader
or a braille display follows, and on the end of the filter when nothing is
listed.
Nothing in a picker is timed unless asked for: the window switcher commits on
a pause only with
.Ic [switcher] commit No = Qq pause .
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic project Oo Ar directory Oo Fl -detached Oc Oc Op Fl -print
Live sessions and the directories the configured source knows, in one list,
each in its project's colour.
The source is
.Xr zoxide 1
by default and
.Ic [project] dirs_source
changes it; a source that is not installed lists nothing rather than failing,
and a directory it has never seen can still be typed in full.
Picking a live session switches to it; picking a directory builds its session
from
.Ic [[layout]] .
A live session nobody is attached to that has been quiet for a day or more
carries an
.Ql idle
column with its age, so the stale ones stand out without leaving the list;
.Ic sessions idle
is the one that closes them.
.Fl -print
lists the rows and exits, and opens nothing; it cannot be combined with a
directory.
Each row is kind, name, path, colour and host, tab-separated, the host empty
for this machine and a host's path shortened against its home.
A host's group line prints as
.Ql host ,
its sessions as
.Ql session
rows named
.Ql api@vm ,
and its directories, inside one of its mirror sessions, as
.Ql dir
rows named the same way; see
.Sx OTHER HOSTS .
With a
.Ar directory ,
.Fl -detached
makes its session and prints the session's name, switching and attaching
nothing: what a mirror session's pane runs on its host before the attach.
Bound to
.Ql M-s .
Sessions are listed most recently attached first, so the second row is the
session you were in before this one, and
.Ql M-s
in the picker switches to it at once:
.Ql M-s M-s
goes back.
It does nothing when there is no other session.
.It Ic project save Op Fl -no-commands
Capture this session's windows and panes as the layout this project opens
with.
A saved layout wins over the config.
The save is all or nothing: if tmux cannot be read, or answers with a line this
cannot parse, nothing is written and the layout already on disk is left alone,
because a layout quietly missing a pane is worse than one that is out of date.
The file is written through a temporary name and renamed over the old one, so a
save interrupted halfway leaves the previous layout rather than half of a new
one.
Bound to
.Ql prefix S .
.It Ic project forget Op Ar directory
Delete the saved layout, so the config decides again.
The project is the session this runs in, or the directory given.
Bound to
.Ql prefix M-S .
.It Ic project show Op Ar directory
Which layout this project gets, which file decided, and the windows it opens.
The order is a saved layout, then the checkout's own
.Pa .tmux-companion.toml ,
then
.Ic [[project.override]] ,
then
.Ic [project] layout .
A saved file that is there and not used is named with the reason, whether it
does not parse or an older build wrote it wrong, and so is a checkout file;
a checkout file whose commands were blanked because the project is not under
.Ic [project] trusted
says so.
The project is the session this runs in, or the directory given.
.Pp
.Ic close-project
is the old name for
.Ic project close
and still works for one release.
.It Ic sessions save Oo Fl -skip-pane-history Oc Op Fl -exclude Ar names
Capture every session on the server as a new generation under the state
directory.
Where
.Ic project save
keeps one file per project, overwritten on demand and edited by hand, this
keeps generations of the whole server and throws the oldest away: the first
answers what a project looks like, the second what you were doing at the time.
The save is all or nothing, like a project's: a line of tmux output this cannot
read stops the write rather than shrinking the snapshot.
A note left on a pane with
.Ic note
is captured with it and put back by a restore.
.Fl -skip-pane-history
records the sessions and leaves out what was on each pane's screen, which is the
expensive half and the half holding whatever you printed.
.Fl -exclude
adds to
.Ic [sessions] exclude
rather than replacing it, and a session left out is one that does not come back.
.It Ic sessions export Oo Ar file Oc Op Fl -stamp Ar stamp
A generation as one file that reads on another machine: every path under the
home directory is spelled
.Ql ~ ,
the project colours from
.Pa _project-map.tsv
travel with it, and what was on each pane's screen is left behind.
The newest generation unless
.Fl -stamp
names one; printed when no file is given.
.It Ic sessions import Ar file Op Fl -no-map
The file
.Ic sessions export
wrote, stored here as a new generation with this machine's home in place of
.Ql ~ ,
and the project colours it names added for the projects this machine has not
coloured yet;
.Fl -no-map
leaves the map alone.
Then
.Ic sessions resurrect
brings it up and says which directories are not here.
The themes themselves come from
.Ic theme init .
.It Ic sessions resurrect Oo Ar stamp Oc Oo Fl -only Ar names Oc Oo Fl -exclude Ar names Oc Oo Fl -merge Oc Oo Fl -dry-run Oc Oo Fl -yes Oc Op Fl -detach
Rebuild a server from a generation, defaulting to the newest.
With no generation of its own to read, it reads tmux-resurrect's newest save
instead, so a machine with months of those loses none of them on the day it
switches over.
A snapshot written by a newer build than this one is read anyway, with one line
on standard error saying the restore may miss what that build knew.
Refuses outright when the server already holds sessions, naming them, because a
restore that half-landed on work somebody was doing is the failure worth being
careful about.
.Fl -merge
adds the sessions that are missing and never touches one that is running.
.Pp
What each pane gets told to run comes from
.Ic [[restore.program]] ,
which is default deny: a command no row claims opens the pane and says so
rather than running it.
So does a command the capture had to guess at, since its arguments are already
gone and running it would bring an agent back in the wrong conversation.
.Fl -dry-run
prints the exact tmux commands and exits, and they are the commands that would
run rather than a description of them.
It prints the waits too, as comments, because a restore is not only tmux calls.
.Pp
When the restore does not know what a pane should run, and somebody is watching,
it shows what it is about to do and counts down before going ahead.
It opens for a pane no row claims, one a row refused, and one whose command the
capture read off a running process so the arguments are already gone.
It opens too when the daemon before this one crashed, once: a restore that goes
ahead clears the crash, and a dry run or a cancel leaves it for the next.
It never opens on a count of panes: a restore where everything is known goes
straight through however many there are, because a confirmation on every restore
is one people turn off.
Any key stops the clock,
.Ql tab
runs one of the listed panes after all,
.Ql a
takes all of them and
.Ql q
cancels.
.Ic [sessions] confirm_secs
sets the countdown and zero never draws it.
.Ic [sessions] confirm_wait
draws it with no countdown and waits for a key, whatever
.Ic confirm_secs
says.
.Fl -yes
runs everything the table claimed without asking; whatever is not run still
opens its pane, in the right directory, at a prompt.
.Pp
Before a pane is told to run anything it has to have drawn a prompt, which tmux
knows only when the shell tells it, which is what
.Ic shell-init
makes the shell do.
A shell that emits the mark answers in milliseconds; one with no
.Ic shell-init
line in its rc file costs two seconds and is then typed into anyway, which is
what a restore did before the marks existed.
.Pp
It attaches at the end when somebody is watching, which means stdin is a
terminal and
.Ev TMUX
is unset, so the same command in a boot script leaves the server running.
.Fl -detach
says so explicitly.
The exit codes are
.Ql 0
restored,
.Ql 2
bad arguments,
.Ql 3
refused because sessions are live,
.Ql 4
nothing to restore, and
.Ql 1
for anything else.
.It Ic sessions shutdown Oo Fl -exclude Ar names Oc Oo Fl -daemon-too Oc Op Fl -dry-run
Save every session, then stop the tmux server.
.Fl -exclude
is not "leave these alone": stopping the server takes every session with it
either way, so a session left out here is one that does not come back, and the
command says which before it acts.
.Fl -daemon-too
stops the tmux-companion daemon as well.
.It Ic sessions restart Oo Fl -exclude Ar names Oc Oo Fl -keep-daemon Oc Op Fl -dry-run
The same, and then bring the server back with what it had.
It stops the daemon by default, which
.Fl -keep-daemon
turns off.
The default is that way round because
.Pa config.toml
is read once when the daemon starts and held for its whole life, so a restart
that left it running would hand back a new binary, a freshly sourced
.Pa tmux.conf ,
and yesterday's configuration.
.Pp
Both refuse to run from inside tmux, because stopping the server would take the
pane they were typed into and nothing after that would run.
There is no flag for it.
Both say so on standard error, as they do when no tmux server is running,
and a
.Ic sessions restart
with no server to restart points at
.Ic sessions resurrect
instead.
.It Ic sessions autosave Oo Fl -once Oc Op Fl -status
The snapshot timer the daemon runs, which
.Ic [sessions] autosave
turns on.
.Fl -once
takes one now and
.Fl -status
says when the last one happened, what the timer is set to, and whether the
daemon before this one stopped cleanly.
With neither flag it reports, the same as
.Fl -status .
.Pp
That last answer comes from a file the daemon writes when it starts and removes
when it stops cleanly; the next daemon to start finds one naming a pid that is
no longer alive and sets it aside as
.Pa crashed ,
which is what the answer reads, so a daemon that is alive right now never
counts as a crash.
The marker stays until a restore has gone ahead on it, or a later crash
replaces it.
A snapshot's own record cannot answer it: a capture taken on the timer says
.Ql taken while running
because the daemon does not know yet, and nothing goes back to correct the one
that turned out to be the last before a crash.
.It Ic sessions list Op Fl -json
Every generation, newest first, with how much each holds and whether the daemon
that wrote it went on to shut down cleanly.
The newest carries a
.Ql *  .
A generation is stamped in UTC, so on a machine well away from it the stamp and
the file's own modification time are hours apart and both are right.
.It Ic sessions show Oo Ar stamp Oc Op Fl -json
What one generation holds, down to each pane's directory and command, defaulting
to the newest.
The command is marked with how sure the capture was of it:
.Ql Exact
for what the pane was started with,
.Ql Guessed
for what it happened to be running, and a shell for a pane sitting at a prompt.
.It Ic sessions rename Ar new Op Fl -session Ar name
Rename a session, the one this runs in unless
.Fl -session
names another, and its project colour with it.
On a host, the grouped session each machine's mirror attached through
becomes
.Ql new~ Ns Ar machine
in the same step, and each machine's mirror session follows on its own as
.Ql new@host .
Run inside such a grouped session, it renames the session the mirror
stands for.
Run on a mirror session here, it says to rename it on its host instead.
A name another session has is refused and nothing moves.
tmux's own
.Ql prefix $
keeps doing what it does, and inside a mirror it ends the same way: see
.Sx OTHER HOSTS .
.It Ic sessions idle Oo Fl -days Ar n Oc Op Fl -print
Every live session with no client attached and nothing happening in it for
longer than
.Ar n
days, three by default, most idle first: the name, the directory, how long it
has sat and how many windows it holds.
Activity is what tmux counts, output in any window included, so a detached
session with a job still printing is not idle.
Picking one runs
.Ic project close
on it, layout capture and all, so a session shut from here opens again from the
project picker as it was.
.Fl -print
lists the rows as tab-separated columns and exits.
Without
.Fl -print ,
inside tmux, the line saying there is nothing to show also goes to the tmux
message line, so a popup that closes does not take it with it.
No such session is one line on stderr and exit 0, not an error.
.It Ic sessions start Ar name Oo Ar dir Oc Op Fl -detach
Open a session by its name.
.Ar name Ns Ql @ Ns Ar host ,
where
.Ar host
is one of
.Ic hosts ,
opens the mirror session of
.Ar name
on that host, described under
.Sx OTHER HOSTS ,
and makes
.Ar name
there first when the host has no session by it, in
.Ar dir
on the host when one is given, where
.Ql ~
is the host's home; a
.Ar dir
the host doesn't have makes nothing and the mirror pane says so.
This works whatever the
host's link mode, since
.Ql off
stops the list and not the attach.
.Ar host
is matched ignoring case when only one host fits, so
.Ql dev2
finds
.Ql Dev2 .
A
.Ar host
that is no host is refused, exit 1 with the hosts named, rather than
read as a local name; only a live session here by that very name still
attaches.
Otherwise it takes the first of these that knows the name:
a live session called
.Ar name ,
which it attaches to, or switches to from inside tmux;
the newest snapshot generation holding a session called
.Ar name ,
which it restores alone, as
.Ic sessions resurrect Fl -only Ar name Fl -merge
would, confirm screen and restore table included;
a project directory whose session would be called
.Ar name ,
from the directories with a saved layout and the ones
.Ic [project] dirs_source
lists, opened the way
.Ic project
opens it;
and last
.Ar dir ,
which gets a session called
.Ar name .
Several project directories with the same name open the project picker on just
those.
With none of these the project picker asks for the directory, and a typed path
is taken as well as a listed one.
Off a terminal there is nobody to ask, so it says the directory is missing and
exits 2.
A
.Ar dir
given when something earlier answered is not used, and one line on standard
error says so.
tmux keeps a
.Ql \&.
or
.Ql \&:
in a name as
.Ql _ ,
and the name is matched the same way.
.Fl -detach
starts or restores the session and attaches nothing.
The exit codes are
.Ql 0
there,
.Ql 2
a directory missing or not a directory, the restore's own codes when a restore
refused, and
.Ql 1
for anything else, a cancelled pick included.
.It Ic sessions continue Op Fl -last , Fl -print
Go back to the session you left: attach to it, or switch to it from inside
tmux, where the session this runs in is never the answer.
tmux cannot say which session a client detached from, since by the time its
.Ql client-detached
hook runs the client has no session, so the record is the one taken on the way
in: the
.Ql @tc_ses_used
stamp the switcher's hooks put on a session at every attach and switch.
This sets those hooks too, as
.Ic switch
does, so on a server whose config does not set them the record starts with the
first
.Ic sessions continue .
Of the sessions no client is in, or of every other session when each has a
client, it takes the highest stamp; with no stamp, the latest
.Ql session_last_attached ;
with neither, the oldest by
.Ql session_created .
.Fl -last
reads none of these and takes the youngest session by
.Ql session_created ,
attached or not, other than the one this runs in.
.Fl -print
prints the name and the reason, a tab between them, and exits.
No other session to go to is one line on standard error and exit 1.
.It Ic shutdown
Stop the tmux-companion daemon, and only that.
tmux and its sessions are not touched, and the next client starts a new
daemon.
.Ic sessions shutdown
is the one that stops tmux.
.It Ic restart
Stop the daemon and start a fresh one, which is how a change to
.Pa config.toml
takes effect: the file is read once at startup and held for the daemon's whole
life.
tmux is not touched;
.Ic sessions restart
is the one that restarts it.
Exits non-zero when the new daemon does not answer, which is what a config it
refuses looks like; the reason is in the daemon log.
.It Ic keys Op Fl -all , Fl -query Ar text , Fl -refresh , Fl -print , Fl -unused
Every binding tmux knows, searchable, with what it runs in the preview.
Enter runs the binding.
It opens on the bindings whose note starts with
.Ql "companion: " ,
which is what the shipped configs write with
.Fl N ;
.Fl -all
opens on everything, including a
.Ql prefix
or
.Ql root
binding with no note, which a plugin's often is; its command stands in for
the note,
.Fl -query
opens on something else,
.Fl -refresh
rebuilds the list from tmux rather than using what the daemon holds, and
.Fl -print
lists the rows and exits.
Without
.Fl -print ,
inside tmux, the line saying there is nothing to show also goes to the tmux
message line, so a popup that closes does not take it with it.
When no binding carries the note there is nothing to show, and it says so on
standard error and exits 0 rather than drawing an empty picker.
Bound to
.Ql prefix ? .
.Pp
.Fl -unused
narrows the opening set to the bindings the usage log has never recorded a
press for, under the title
.Ql "[ Keys never used ]" .
Never means since the log began, and the line under the list says how many
bindings that is out of how many, and how many presses the log holds; with no
log yet it says so and lists every binding, which is the truth on a fresh
machine.
With
.Fl -print
it writes one
.Ar table key note
line per binding to standard output and the same summary to standard error.
It does not combine with
.Fl -all :
tmux's own bindings are nobody's to prune.
.It Ic keys discover Op Fl -layer Ar layer , Fl -print
Reads what tmux's root table binds and what the apps in its panes bind, and
saves it for
.Ic keys collide .
No app is asked to do anything new.
Each app is read by the best way that answers: a running instance through an
interface it already has, then its own configuration or a headless start with
it, then what
.Ic [keys.app. Ns Ar name Ns Ic ] claims
declares.
nvim is asked live over the socket every nvim serves, one call to
.Fn nvim_get_keymap
per mode and nothing else, and every nvim pane answers for itself; when none
does, one
.Ql nvim --headless
start with your config answers instead.
Only keys tmux's root table could stand in front of are kept: Ctrl, Alt and
the F keys.
claude is read from its
.Pa keybindings.json
over a table of its default keys, fzf from
.Ev FZF_DEFAULT_OPTS
over fzf's defaults, vim from
.Ql :map
in a silent start with your vimrc, nano from the
.Ql bind
lines of your nanorc with no defaults, since nothing installed lists them,
and zsh from
.Ql bindkey -L
in an interactive start; zsh is reported and never routed, since it is the
command in every idle pane.
fzf-lua's fzf keys come back from a live nvim when fzf-lua is loaded.
.Fl -layer
reads only one of
.Ql tmux ,
.Ql nvim ,
.Ql vim ,
.Ql claude ,
.Ql fzf ,
.Ql nano
or
.Ql zsh ,
and
.Fl -print
writes one
.Ar layer mode key rung pane description source
line per binding as well.
What could not be read goes to standard error, one line per layer.
It runs in the client; the daemon never starts or connects to an editor.
.It Ic keys collide Op Fl -json , Fl -refresh
The keys tmux's root table binds that an app binds too, with every app that
wants each one and the modes it wants it in, followed by the Ctrl, Alt and F
keys nobody binds, for when a new shared action needs a home.
Two spellings of one key, such as
.Ql M-S-a
and
.Ql M-A ,
count as one.
A key the registry describes, with a field naming a layer and a word or two
its binding should contain, is listed as drift when that layer binds it to
something else or to nothing; collisions show the route the registry gives
them.
It reads the last
.Ic keys discover ,
and discovers first when there is none or with
.Fl -refresh .
.Fl -json
prints the report instead of opening the picker.
.It Ic keys route Op Fl -dry-run , Fl -print
Wraps tmux's root bindings so that a key the app in front of a pane wants is
held rather than taken.
It belongs on the last line of tmux.conf,
.Ql run-shell \(dqtmux-companion keys route\(dq ,
because it reads the root table as tmux.conf left it.
Each Ctrl, Alt or F binding in root moves, untouched and with its note, into
the
.Ql kc-tmux
table, and root gets a wrapper carrying the same note.
When nothing claims the key it runs the binding at once.
When something does, it waits
.Ic [keys] hold_ms ,
170 by default: a second press in that time sends one key to the app, and
when the time runs out, or another key comes first, tmux runs its binding and
the other key follows.
A claim is the pane-option contract below, or a static claim: what
.Ic keys discover
found for the app in front, in the modes that count, plus
.Ic [keys.app. Ns Ar name Ns Ic ] claims .
A key whose name has punctuation in it is left alone.
.Ic [keys.key. Ns Ar key Ns Ic ] route
settles one key:
.Ql hold ,
the default,
.Ql app ,
which hands a claimed key to the app at once and runs tmux's binding at once
otherwise, the way vim-tmux-navigator shares its keys, or
.Ql tmux ,
which leaves the key unwrapped; its
.Ic hold_ms
overrides the one in
.Ic [keys] .
With
.Ic [keys] route
off, the default, it puts every wrapped binding back and does nothing else;
below tmux 3.4 it says so and wraps nothing.
Running it again after tmux.conf is sourced wraps the fresh bindings.
It prints nothing unless something fails;
.Fl -print
says what was wrapped and what was left alone, and
.Fl -dry-run
prints the tmux script as well and applies nothing.
.It Ic keys claim Oo Fl -app Ar name Oc Oo Fl -owner Oc Oo Fl t Ar pane Oc Op Ar key ...
Sets the pane options a wrapper reads, spelled the way it matches them, for a
hook or a wrapper script.
The keys go into
.Ql @kc_claim ;
.Fl -app
sets
.Ql @kc_app ,
for an app whose process name doesn't say what it is;
.Fl -owner
records the pane's current command as
.Ql @kc_owner ,
so the claim stops counting once something else is in front.
The pane is
.Ev TMUX_PANE
unless
.Fl t
names one.
.It Ic keys release Op Fl t Ar pane
Unsets all three.
.Pp
The contract, for anybody writing a publisher: code inside an app that keeps
its claims current as its mode changes.
.Ql @kc_owner
is the
.Ql pane_current_command
tmux shows for the app;
.Ql @kc_claim
is the keys claimed right now in tmux's spelling, each between pipes, with a
pipe at both ends:
.Ql |M-a|M-1|C-q| .
A claim counts only while the pane's command equals
.Ql @kc_owner ,
so one left behind by an app that crashed stops counting once the shell is
back.
While
.Ql @kc_owner
names the app in front, its static claims don't apply: a publisher that
claims nothing in this mode unsets
.Ql @kc_claim
and means it.
.It Ic cheatsheet Op Fl -print
The bindings you keep looking up, so you learn them and stop.
A pick in the
.Ic keys
picker is a binding looked up rather than pressed, and with
.Ic [usage] enabled
on the log records each one with its time.
A binding looked up within the last
.Ic [usage] learned_after_days ,
14 by default, is still being learned: it goes at the top of its box with a
.Ql \[u25B8]
and its count, most lookups first.
One with lookups but none that recent has been learned and leaves the sheet;
the line under it says how many did.
0 days means nothing is ever learned.
A pick logged before picks carried a time counts as learned until it is
looked up again.
.Pp
Three boxes hold the bindings noted
.Ql "companion: " ,
by the word after it: panes, windows, sessions and projects; copy mode,
opening and searching; config, help and anything else.
The ones never looked up follow the marked ones, alphabetical.
The fourth box, tmux and plugins, holds bindings without that note, and only
ones looked up recently: tmux's own show tmux's note, and one with no note
shows its command.
A default nobody looked up is never listed.
.Pp
With the usage log off, every
.Ql "companion: "
binding is shown unranked, the fourth box is empty, and the line under the
sheet says it learns only with the log on.
.Pp
.Fl -print
writes one line per entry instead of drawing the sheet:
.Ar box state count table key shown note ,
tab-separated, where
.Ar box
is 1 to 4 in reading order and
.Ar state
is
.Ql learn
or
.Ql unused .
The line under the sheet goes to standard error.
.Fl -plain
is the old spelling and still works.
Without
.Fl -print ,
inside tmux, the line saying there is nothing to show also goes to the tmux
message line, so a popup that closes does not take it with it.
The same hint as
.Ic keys
when no binding carries the note.
Bound to
.Ql prefix C-c .
.It Ic agent Cm busy | asked | done Op Fl -pane Ar id
What an agent is doing, said by the agent itself.
Run from one of its hooks, it tells the daemon that the agent in
.Ev $TMUX_PANE
is working
.Pq Cm busy :
a prompt was sent or a tool just ran ,
has stopped on a question or a permission prompt
.Pq Cm asked ,
or has finished its answer and waits for the next prompt
.Pq Cm done .
The last word per pane wins over the window's quiet time everywhere the
daemon reads it: the bar's
.Ic agents
segment, the inbox, the
.Ic panes
picker and the brief, and it lasts as long as the pane runs one of
.Ic [agents] programs .
Nothing is printed and the exit is 0 whatever happens, since the command runs
inside the agent's own hook and an error there would land in its transcript;
outside tmux it does nothing.
.Fl -pane
names another pane.
.It Ic agent clear Op Fl -pane Ar id
Forget what the pane last said, so the window's quiet time decides again:
for an agent restarted without its hooks in a pane that still carries the
old one's last word.
.It Ic agent hooks Op Ar program
Print the hooks block for an agent's settings file, the way
.Ic shell-init
prints the prompt marks.
For
.Ql claude ,
the only program so far, that is
.Ql UserPromptSubmit
and
.Ql PostToolUse
saying busy,
.Ql Stop
saying done, and
.Ql Notification
on a permission prompt or
.Ql PreToolUse
on
.Ql AskUserQuestion
saying asked, and
.Ql SessionStart
and
.Ql SessionEnd
running
.Ic keys claim --app claude --owner
and
.Ic keys release ,
so key routing knows a pane whose process is named for claude's version is
claude; all of it to merge into
.Pa ~/.claude/settings.json .
.Ql idle_prompt
is left out on purpose: it fires a minute after every answer and would turn
each
.Cm done
into a question.
Between a hook and silence sits the terminal bell: an agent that rings it
sets the window's bell flag until the window is visited, and an agent pane
with the flag up reads as asked.
.It Ic inbox Op Fl -print
The agents waiting on you, longest wait first, each with the question it
asked: the daemon captures the last lines of an agent's screen the moment it
stops drawing, or the moment its hook says
.Cm asked
or
.Cm done ,
so the question is on record for a window nobody has looked at since.
The third column says how it stopped and for how long:
.Ql asked 3m
for a hook or the bell,
.Ql done 3m
for a hook that said the answer was complete, which is on the list to be read
rather than answered and is not coloured, and
.Ql waiting 3m
for silence alone.
Enter switches the attached client to that pane.
.Fl -print
writes where, program, state and how long, the question and the pane id as
tab-separated columns.
Without
.Fl -print ,
inside tmux, the line saying there is nothing to show also goes to the tmux
message line, so a popup that closes does not take it with it.
The question is the last line of the screen that is something the agent
said: the input box at the foot of the screen and whatever is under it are
cut, and then every line matching one of
.Ic [agents] question_skip ,
a list of regular expressions that ships covering rules, spinners, hints and a
dialog's numbered options.
.Ic [agents] inbox
turns the capture off, and
.Ic [agents] nudge_after_secs
says it out loud once an agent has waited that long, unless it said
.Cm done ,
which is an answer and not a question.
.It Ic panes Op Fl -agents , Fl -print , Fl t Ar session
Every pane on the server, one row each: where it is as
.Ql session:window.pane ,
what it runs, whether it has gone quiet, and its directory, with the last
lines of its screen in the preview.
Enter switches the attached client to that pane, whatever session it is in.
The pane this runs in is left out, since jumping to where you are is nothing.
The state column reads
.Ql busy ,
.Ql asked 3m ,
.Ql done 3m
or
.Ql waiting 3m
for a pane running one of
.Ic [agents] programs ,
the first three when the agent said so through
.Ic agent
or rang the bell and the last when only the window's quiet time is known,
.Ql active
or
.Ql idle 3m
for anything else, and
.Ql reading
for a pane in copy mode.
Quiet is measured on the window rather than the pane, because tmux keeps no
activity time per pane: an agent beside a shell you are typing in reads as
busy.
The program column carries the pane's title after a dash when a program set
one, which is how two
.Ql claude
panes in one directory are told apart.
.Fl -agents
keeps only the agent rows,
.Fl t
only one session's, and
.Fl -print
writes the rows as tab-separated columns with the pane id last, and exits.
Without
.Fl -print ,
inside tmux, the line saying there is nothing to show also goes to the tmux
message line, so a popup that closes does not take it with it.
With nothing to show it says so on standard error and exits 0.
Bound to
.Ql prefix g ,
and
.Ql prefix G
with
.Fl -agents .
.It Ic jump Op Fl -pane Ar id , Fl -client Ar name
Go to any text on the screen by typing a few of its characters and then the
label that appears beside it, the motion
.Lk https://github.com/folke/flash.nvim flash.nvim
gives neovim, over every pane in the window.
A borderless popup is laid exactly over the window and shows what each pane
shows, greyed.
As the search is typed, its matches light up in the theme's colour, nearest
the cursor first, and each gets a letter on the cell after it.
No label is ever a letter that would continue a match, so typing on narrows
the search and typing a label jumps, and a match keeps its letter while the
search narrows.
The search ignores case until it holds a capital.
Enter takes the nearest match; escape, or backspace on an empty search,
closes it.
The jump selects the pane, puts it in copy mode and moves the cursor to the
match by row and column, so a selection already started extends to it.
A pane already in copy mode is searched where its view is scrolled to.
.Fl -pane
is the pane the key was pressed in and
.Fl -client
the client to open the popup on, both from the binding.
It replaces
.Lk https://github.com/schasse/tmux-jump tmux-jump ,
which took one character and, on tmux 3.5 and later, landed a column further
right for every line above its target.
Bound to
.Ql prefix Space ,
and to
.Ql s
in copy mode.
.It Ic search Oo Ar pattern Oc Op Fl F , Fl -kind Ar kind , Fl t Ar session , Fl -pane Ar id , Fl -lines Ar n , Fl -print , Fl -first
Every line of every pane's scrollback, one row each and the newest first:
where it is as
.Ql session:window.pane ,
what the pane runs, and the line, with the five lines on either side of it in
the preview.
Enter switches the attached client to that pane, puts it in copy mode
scrolled to the line and selects the line, so what you found is what's
highlighted and the copy key takes it.
The panes are read most recently active first, a blank line is no row, and a
line one pane drew twice is one row at its newest, so a prompt drawn forty
times doesn't fill the list.
.Ar pattern
keeps only the lines it matches.
It's a regular expression, matched in either case unless it holds a capital
letter, and
.Fl F
takes it as text, for an error message full of brackets.
.Fl -kind
is a stored search in place of a pattern:
.Cm url ,
.Cm path ,
.Cm sha
or
.Cm ip .
.Fl t
keeps one session's panes and
.Fl -pane
one pane.
.Fl -lines
is how far back each pane is read, 1000 when nothing says, and the list stops
at 20000 rows.
.Fl -print
writes the rows as tab-separated columns and exits: where, the pane id, the
line's number as tmux counts it, with 0 the top row of the screen and the
history below zero, and the line.
Without
.Fl -print ,
inside tmux, the line saying there is nothing to show also goes to the tmux
message line, so a popup that closes does not take it with it.
.Fl -first
goes to the newest match without showing the list, and needs a pattern or a
kind.
A line is a line the terminal drew, so a URL that wrapped is two of them and
neither matches whole.
What a pane wrote while the list was open moved the line up, so at landing
the pane is read again and the line found by its text, which also holds for a
pane whose history is already at
.Ql history-limit
and can't grow.
The stored searches come from
.Lk https://github.com/tmux-plugins/tmux-copycat tmux-copycat ,
which hasn't been pushed since May 2023, and the fuzzy list over one pane is
.Lk https://github.com/roosta/tmux-fuzzback tmux-fuzzback ,
which is the one to install on a machine that doesn't run this.
Bound to
.Ql prefix / .
.It Ic ports Op Fl -all , Fl -udp , Fl -print , Fl t Ar session , Fl -kill , Fl -grace Ar secs
The TCP ports something is listening on, one row each: the port and its
protocol, where it can be reached from, the program and its pid, the pane that started it as
.Ql session:window.pane ,
and that pane's directory, with the last lines of the pane's screen in the
preview.
Enter switches the attached client to the pane.
The address column reads
.Ql *
for every interface,
.Ql localhost
for either loopback, or the address it is bound to, and a program listening
on one port over IPv4 and IPv6 is one row.
A port belongs to the pane whose own process is an ancestor of the one
listening, so a server started by a script started by an agent is still that
agent's pane's.
Only the ports a pane started are listed;
.Fl -all
adds the rest after them, with a dash for the pane.
.Fl -udp
adds the UDP sockets that are bound and connected to nobody, which is as
near as UDP comes to listening; it's asked for rather than given because any
program that resolves a name holds one for a moment.
.Fl t
keeps one session's, and
.Fl -print
writes the rows as tab-separated columns and exits: port, protocol, address,
program, pid, where, and the pane id.
Without
.Fl -print ,
inside tmux, the line saying there is nothing to show also goes to the tmux
message line, so a popup that closes does not take it with it.
With
.Fl -kill
enter stops the program where it would have gone to the pane: TERM to the
process holding the socket, and KILL if it's still there after
.Fl -grace
seconds, 3 when nothing says.
Only that process is signalled and not its group, since what started it is
often the pane's shell.
The sockets come from
.Xr lsof 8 ,
or from
.Xr ss 8
on a machine without it, and either one names only the processes you own
unless you're root.
Bound to
.Ql prefix P .
.It Ic new-window Op Fl -print
A window here, or at any directory.
The rows are the pane's own directory and then the same source
.Ic project
lists, so it is the one setting for both.
The query starts on the pane's own directory, which is also the first row, so
the key and enter is another window here.
A path the source has never seen opens when it is typed in full.
.Fl -print
writes the rows and exits: a column that says
.Ql here
on the pane's own directory, the short path and the full one.
Bound to
.Ql prefix c .
.It Ic run Op Fl -pane Ar id
A command from shell history, run in a pane that slides out beside this one
and waits for an answer before closing.
.Fl -pane
names the pane the side pane belongs beside.
The shipped binding passes nothing, and then the session an attached client is
looking at decides.
Both exist because the picker draws inside
.Ic display-popup ,
which is not a client: an untargeted split is resolved against whichever
session the server touched last, so the command ran in a window nobody was
looking at.
tmux does not expand
.Ql #{pane_id}
in a
.Ic display-popup
command at all, and expands it in a
.Ic run-shell
one to the pane the server touched last rather than the pane the key was
pressed in, which is why the flag is for a caller that knows the pane and not
for the binding.
Bound to
.Ql prefix e .
.It Ic theme pick Op Fl -themes Ar dir
Every theme with its colour beside it, applied on the spot and remembered
against the project in
.Pa _project-map.tsv .
It goes to the session the key was pressed in, found from
.Ev TMUX_PANE
in a pane and from the session
.Ev TMUX
names in a popup;
.Fl t
applies it somewhere else.
A session in a group is the group: every session in it is painted, the pick
is remembered under the group's name, and a grouped session made later takes
the group's theme from the
.Ql session-created
hook.
That is what makes a pick inside a mirror session reach the host session for
everybody, since the mirror attaches through a grouped session whose bar is
off.
Each client looking at the group is told
.Ql theme Amber on api
on its message line, a mirror's excepted.
The session also gets
.Ql @theme-file ,
the theme's file name, which
.Ql @theme-name
is not.
Bound to
.Ql prefix C-t .
.El
.Sh SESSIONS AND WINDOWS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic note Oo Ar text Oc Op Fl -pane Ar id , Fl -clear
Leave a one-line note on a pane: tmux's pane title, which
.Ic panes
shows beside the program and the pane border draws when
.Ic pane-border-status
is on.
With no text it prints the note that is there;
.Fl -clear
takes it off.
A snapshot carries the note and a restore puts it back.
The pane defaults to the one the key was pressed in.
An agent working beside you can say what it is doing in four words with this,
and the skill file tells it to.
.It Ic quiet Oo Ar duration Oc Oo Fl -off Oc Oo Fl -focus Op Ar name | Fl -focus-off Oc
Quiet hours.
For the duration given,
.Ql 45m ,
.Ql 2h
or a bare number of minutes,
.Ic [notify]
announces nothing, the inbox nudges nobody, and the
.Ic agents
segment leaves the bar; the health mark is a crossed-out bell in its
place, so the silence reads as chosen rather than broken, and
.Ic health
says
.Ql quiet
in words.
With nothing given it says how long is left;
.Fl -off
ends it now.
.Ic [quiet] daily
turns it on at the same local times every day, such as
.Ql 22:00-08:00 ;
a timer and a window together last until the later one ends.
The daemon keeps the clock, so every timer and every client agree.
.Pp
.Fl -focus Op Ar name
says a Focus started and
.Fl -focus-off
that it ended: quiet holds until then, and
.Ic quiet
and
.Ic health
say
.Ql quiet while Focus Work is on .
They are for a hook to call; on macOS that is a Shortcuts automation,
.Dq When Work turns on ,
running
.Ql tmux-companion quiet --focus Work
through Run Shell Script with the binary's full path, and a second for
.Dq turns off .
.Fl -off
ends the timer and leaves a Focus on.
With
.Ic [quiet] follow_focus
on macOS the daemon follows the Focus itself, from the database in
.Pa ~/Library/DoNotDisturb/DB :
one stat every two seconds and a read when it changed.
Reading it can need Full Disk Access for the terminal tmux runs in; when it
cannot be read the health mark says so once and quiet keeps to the timer and
.Ic daily .
On Linux the same setting follows the desktop's Do Not Disturb over the
session bus, one connection and its signals, with no timer and no process:
GNOME's
.Ql show-banners ,
read from the dconf user database when dconf says it was written,
KDE Plasma's
.Ql Inhibited ,
dunst's
.Ql paused ,
mako's
.Ql do-not-disturb
mode and SwayNotificationCenter's Do Not Disturb.
The line is then
.Ql quiet while Do Not Disturb is on ,
as it is for the Do Not Disturb Focus on macOS.
No session bus is one health reason.
.It Ic chunk Op Cm status Oo Fl -json Oc | Cm snooze | Cm reset | Cm close | Cm budget Ar duration
The chunk clock: how long you have been at tmux this sitting, across every
session, with nothing to start.
Time counts while an attached client has the OS focus, which needs
.Ic focus-events on ,
and saw a key or click within
.Ic [chunk] break_after ;
a shorter gap counts as work, and one that reaches it is a break that ends the
sitting.
The
.Ic chunk
segment of
.Ic [[status.right.segments]]
draws a timer and
.Ql 38m ,
amber past
.Ic [chunk] warn_at
of the budget, and an alert timer and
.Ql 50m+12
in red past it, with
.Ql \(bu 1h14m
after either when the pane in front runs an agent, how long it has been
running, unless
.Ic [chunk] agent_age
is off.
At budget the daemon plays the
.Ql chunk
earcon once, at the next window switch, prompt coming back or agent finishing
its turn, and at most
.Ic [chunk] boundary_grace
late; quiet hours keep the sound and drop the
.Ic [chunk] banner .
.Cm status ,
the default, says the sitting in words,
.Ql 38 min, 12 min for break
or
.Ql 1 hr 7 min, 17 min passed break time ,
and nothing when no sitting runs;
.Ic brief
says the same line.
.Cm snooze ,
past the budget, grants one extension of
.Ic [chunk] snooze
and one more sound, once a sitting.
.Cm reset
starts a new sitting at 0,
.Cm close
ends this one until you have been away and back, and
.Cm budget
sets this sitting's budget until a reset or a daemon restart.
The sitting survives a daemon restart in
.Pa $XDG_STATE_HOME/tmux-companion/chunk.toml .
A terminal that does not report focus reads as focused forever, and there only
the key counts.
.It Ic journal Oo Fl -print Oc Oo Fl t Ar session Oc Op Fl -days Ar n
What happened in each project, newest first: a command that ran past
.Ic [journal] min_secs
and finished, with how long it took; an agent that stopped and what it asked;
an agent that worked one turn past
.Ic [journal] agent_min_secs
and said it was done, with how long and the last line of its answer; a
project opened or closed.
Enter goes to that session.
.Fl t
keeps one session and
.Fl -days
reaches further back than the last day.
.Fl -print
writes the rows as tab-separated columns.
Without
.Fl -print ,
inside tmux, the line saying there is nothing to show also goes to the tmux
message line, so a popup that closes does not take it with it.
The daemon writes it to
.Pa journal.tsv
in the state directory as it happens.
It is off by default.
.It Ic switch windows Oo Ar session Oc Op Fl -reverse , Fl -client Ar name , Fl -print
Move between this session's windows in the order they were used, the way
Cmd-Tab moves between applications.
Run from
.Ic run-shell ,
with no terminal, it is one press of the key.
With
.Ic [switcher] first
set to
.Ql flip ,
the default, the first press goes to the window you were on before and
draws nothing, once
.Ic [switcher] flip_wait_ms ,
150 milliseconds unless set, has gone without a second press.
A second press inside that wait opens a popup listing the windows, most
recently used first, with nothing moved and the candidate on the window you
were on before;
a second press after it but within
.Ic [switcher] pause_ms ,
500 milliseconds unless set, opens the list over the window the flip went
to, on the next older one.
.Ic flip_wait_ms No = 0
flips at once.
With
.Ql list ,
the first press opens that list and nothing moves until a commit.
Inside the list the same key moves on and, with shift, back;
Enter commits, and Esc goes back to the window the presses began in.
With
.Ic [switcher] commit
set to
.Ql pause ,
the list also commits
.Ic pause_ms
after the last press.
Typing filters the list, and stops that clock.
The window you are in carries the word
.Ql now .
With
.Ic [switcher] announce ,
on by default, each press puts the candidate's name and its place, such as
.Ql ai, 2 of 4 ,
on the message line.
.Fl -reverse
goes the other way,
.Fl -client
is the client the key was pressed on, which the binding passes as
.Ql #{client_name} ,
and
.Fl -print
prints the windows, index and name, most recently used first, one to a
line.
In a mirror session the windows are the host's: the current one first, then
the rest by index, as the daemon last heard them over the link, or a line
saying they haven't been heard yet followed by the mirror session's own
window.
Run in a terminal, such as a popup, it is the list.
.Pp
The order comes from hooks that only set options, so a switch starts no
process:
.Ql session-window-changed[73] ,
.Ql client-session-changed[73]
and
.Ql client-attached[73]
count every switch into
.Ql @tc_seq
and stamp the window or session with it as
.Ql @tc_win_used
or
.Ql @tc_ses_used .
.Ic switch
sets them each time it runs.
A window never stamped comes after every stamped one, tmux's last window
first.
.Pp
Under
.Ql flip
the second press opening the list is timed, and so is the commit under
.Ic commit No = Qq pause .
.Ic [switcher] first No = Qq list
with
.Ic commit No = Qq enter
has no clock in it at all.
Bound to
.Ql M-a ,
and with
.Fl -reverse
to
.Ql M-A ,
in the example configs.
.It Ic switch sessions Op Fl -reverse , Fl -print
The project picker opened as a session switcher: every session, most
recently used first, with the cursor on the one you were in before, or with
.Fl -reverse
on the oldest.
The session key, Alt held, moves on and with shift back, Enter goes and Esc
stays; there is no clock.
The project directories are in the list but hidden until something is typed,
so the first letter turns it into the project picker's search, and a typed
path opens as it does there.
A search filters without reordering: the sessions stay first, by when each was
last used, and the projects follow, by when each was last jumped to, read from
zoxide's database; another source keeps its own order.
.Pp
After this machine's sessions comes each host's, in the order
.Ic hosts
lists them: a line for the host,
.Ql vm, 4 sessions, last heard 15m ,
or
.Ql live
while its link is up, then each of its sessions as
.Ql api@vm ,
in the host's own order of use, with
.Ql idle 3d
and
.Ql attached on vm
as a local row has them.
In the list a host's line shows only while nothing else stands for the host:
once it has a session row, or a mirror session open here, those rows are the
host, each with the ssh glyph and
.Ql @vm
in the host's colour, and the preview of a session says the host's line.
.Fl -print
keeps every host's line.
The rows come from what the daemon last heard, kept on disk, so they are
there before any ssh has run; opening the list asks the daemon to bring each
host's link up afterwards, and nothing in the list waits for it.
While a host can't be reached its rows stay, each saying
.Ql unreachable
and drawn in a faint tone, and the preview of any of them says why and when
it was last tried.
Enter on a host's line, or on one of those rows, tries the link again and
says
.Ql vm: connecting
on the message line.
The session key steps over host lines and unreachable rows, landing only on
sessions, and the cursor starts on the second session whichever machine it
is on.
The message line says each step as
.Ql api on vm, 5 of 9 .
Enter on a host's session opens it as the host's
.Ic open
says, a mirror session by default, and a typed
.Ql name@vm
that is no session's name gets a row of its own at the top,
.Ql new session name on vm ,
so a loose match further down can't take Enter.
Enter on it asks where on the host the session starts, in a second list
in the same popup: the host's home,
.Ql ~ ,
first, then the directories the host lists, and a typed path as a row
saying
.Ql start in ~/src/x on vm .
Esc makes nothing.
ctrl-o on a host's line, one of its sessions or a mirror session open here
asks for the name first, in a list of the sessions the host already has
with the same kind of row for a new one, then where.
Nothing typed in either goes through tmux's command parser or a shell
here: the name and the directory are arguments, quoted once for the
host's shell.
alt-enter on a host's session opens it the other way, once: it hands the
terminal over for a host that mirrors, and mirrors one that hands over.
A mirror session that is open is listed once, among this machine's sessions.
.Pp
ctrl-x on a host's session row, or on a mirror session open here, asks in
the same popup what it is for, one row each:
.Ql keep api@vm ,
first, so Enter at once does nothing;
.Ql kill api on vm, and every mirror of it ,
which runs
.Ic host kill ;
and
.Ql forget vm ,
which runs
.Ic hosts forget .
For a host that can't be reached the middle row drops the cached row
instead, which the next answer from the host brings back if the session is
still there.
Esc keeps both.
What happened is said on the message line.
On a host's line it closes the list and asks
.Ql forget vm? y/n
on the message line, where one key answers and only
.Ql y
forgets, then opens the list again on the same row; it is refused, with the
reason, while a mirror session to the host is open.
On one of this machine's own sessions it says that ctrl-x is for a host's
rows.
ctrl-n on a host's line, one of its sessions or a mirror session opens the
form
.Ic hosts add
opens with no arguments.
A host whose line is not shown is forgotten with
.Ic hosts forget .
.Pp
.Fl -print
prints the session names, most recently used first, then each host's line
with the reason after
.Ql unreachable\&: ,
and its sessions, the same lines in the same order with no glyph and no
padding.
Bound to
.Ql M-s
and
.Ql M-S
in the example configs.
.It Ic project close Oo Ar session Oc Op Fl -discard , Fl -no-save
Close a project by asking every window to exit, capturing the layout on the
way out.
An editor is asked to quit first, in its own language, and the close stops
with the editor on screen when it will not; the editors it knows are
.Ic nvim , vim , vi
and
.Ic hx .
Everything else, an editor it does not know included, gets
.Ql exit
or ctrl-D.
.Fl -discard
quits editors with
.Ql :qa!
and throws away unsaved work.
A session that is not there is an error rather than a session that closed at
once.
Bound to
.Ql prefix X .
.It Ic pocket Oo Ar name Oc Op Fl -pane Ar id
A shell you pull out beside the pane you are in, look at, and put away
without losing it.
The first press splits a shell off the side of the window at
.Ic [run] width_percent ;
the second parks it in a window called
.Ql _pocket ;
the third brings the same pane back beside wherever you are now, its process
and its scrollback as they were.
.Ar name
tells one pocket from another when a session keeps more than one, and
defaults to
.Ql shell .
A pocket is known by the pane option
.Ql @tmux-companion-pocket
rather than by its title, which a shell is free to change.
.Ic switch windows
passes the
.Ql _pocket
window, and
.Ic project save
leaves it, and any pocket that is out, off the layout it writes.
.Fl -pane
is the pane the key was pressed in, which the binding passes.
Pressed inside the
.Ql _pocket
window it does nothing and says so.
Needs tmux 3.1 for pane options.
Bound to
.Ql prefix ` .
.It Ic promote Oo Ar name Oc Op Fl -pane Ar id
Give a pane a session of its own, its process and its scrollback with it:
for the pane opened beside the editor to look at another repository, which
an hour later is the thing being worked on.
The session is named the way
.Ic project
names one, from the pane's directory, so it is the session
.Ic project
finds the next time that directory is picked.
.Ar name
is for when the directory's name won't do.
When a session of that name is already there the pane joins it as a window,
and when the pane is all its own session holds the session is renamed and
nothing moves.
A pane already in the session it would be given is refused, with a line
saying to pass a name.
The attached client follows the pane, and the journal gets an
.Ql opened
line for a session this made.
.Fl -pane
is the pane the key was pressed in, which the binding passes.
The idea is
.Lk https://github.com/tmux-plugins/tmux-sessionist tmux-sessionist Ns 's
.Ql prefix @ ,
which asked for the name, and it hasn't been pushed since May 2023.
Bound to
.Ql prefix @ .
.It Ic kill Op Fl -pane Ar id , Fl -grace Ar secs , Fl -ask
Stop what runs in front in a pane, for a program that stopped answering
.Ql C-c .
The foreground process group of the pane's terminal gets TERM, which a
program can catch to put the terminal back and remove its lock file, and KILL
when it's still running after
.Fl -grace
seconds, 3 when nothing says.
The group is the one the terminal delivers
.Ql C-c
to, so a build goes together with the compilers it started and a job put in
the background with
.Ql &
is left alone.
It prints how it ended,
.Ql cargo stopped on TERM
or
.Ql cargo ignored TERM for 3s and was killed ,
and exits 1 when the program is still there after KILL or could not be
signalled.
A pane with nothing in front but its own shell is refused, since stopping
that closes the pane and tmux has
.Ql kill-pane
for it.
.Fl -pane
is the pane the key was pressed in, which the binding passes.
.Fl -ask
looks before it asks: a shell at its prompt is refused with no question, and
anything else gets tmux's own confirmation naming the program and its pid,
whose yes runs the stop.
.Lk https://github.com/tmux-plugins/tmux-cowboy tmux-cowboy
was the one key for this, sending KILL with no TERM before it, and was last
pushed in May 2021.
Bound to
.Ql prefix K
with
.Fl -ask ,
so the confirmation names what it stops.
.It Ic zen Op Fl -pane Ar id
Clear everything but the pane you are working in.
With other panes open that is tmux's own zoom: this pane fills the window, the
rest are hidden, and the same key puts the layout back untouched.
With no other panes there is nothing to zoom -- a lone pane already fills the
window, and tmux's own
.Ql prefix z
does nothing at all there -- so the key takes the status bar instead, which is
the only clutter left.
One key for "give me the most screen you can", whatever the window looks like.
.Ic zoom
is the old name and works for one more release.
Bound to
.Ql prefix z .
.It Ic autosave Op Fl -once , Fl -status
Deprecated: the
.Ic [autosave]
script timer, which
.Ic [sessions] autosave
replaces.
.Fl -once
runs the script now,
.Fl -status
says when it last ran, and with no flag it reports, the same as
.Fl -status .
.El
.Sh OTHER HOSTS
A host is another machine with its own tmux and its own tmux-companion,
reached by a command that opens ssh to it.
The daemon keeps a link to each host's daemon: one host command per host,
.Ql ssh vm 'tmux-companion bridge' ,
carrying Cap'n Proto messages over the ssh session's stdin and stdout.
Nothing listens on either machine and nothing new opens a socket; the local
side starts every link and asks every question, and the host answers only the
questions its bridge has, none of which runs a command or changes anything.
Everything a host sends is taken as untrusted: control characters are
dropped, a string is cut at 4 KiB and a list at 500 sessions.
.Pp
While a link is up the host tells this side what changed when it changes:
its sessions, its waiting agents and the bar of each grouped session this
machine has a client in, each sent when the host's own poll sees it change,
so nothing is asked on a timer.
The daemon pings the host every
.Ic ping_secs ,
15 by default, since a link that died quietly would otherwise go unnoticed;
three pings unanswered in a row and the link is restarted, saying
.Ql no answer to 3 pings in 45s .
A host whose bridge is older and can't push is asked for its sessions every
.Ic poll_secs ,
5 by default, instead, and
.Ic hosts
says its bridge lacks
.Ql watch .
The last answer is kept on disk either way, so a host is listed with
when it was last heard from before any ssh has run.
A link that fails is tried again after 5 seconds, then 10, doubling to 5
minutes, and at once when something asks for it.
The link runs with no controlling terminal and
.Ev SSH_ASKPASS_REQUIRE
set to
.Ql never ,
so ssh fails and says why rather than waiting on a password or a host key
nobody can see; host keys stay strict.
Set
.Ql BatchMode yes
and a
.Ql ConnectTimeout
for the host in
.Pa ~/.ssh/config
as well.
.Pp
A host's sessions are listed in
.Ic switch sessions ,
and picking one opens a mirror session: a session here called
.Ql api@vm
that stands for
.Ql api
on
.Ql vm ,
holding one window whose pane runs
.Ic host attach .
That pane runs the host command with
.Fl t
and attaches to a session of its own on the host,
.Ql api~ Ns Ar machine ,
named for this machine and grouped with
.Ql api ,
so this machine keeps its own current window there and two machines don't
collide.
The grouped session carries
.Ql @tc-mirror ,
has its bar off, and goes when this machine detaches.
It sets
.Ql detach-on-destroy on
for itself, whatever the host sets globally: when the last pane of
.Ql api
goes, the attach ends and
.Ql api@vm
closes, rather than the host moving it to another of its sessions while
it is still called
.Ql api@vm .
The host's own pickers,
.Ic brief ,
.Ic sessions idle ,
snapshots and bar leave it out, so a person at the host sees what they saw
before.
Grouped sessions share their windows, and
.Ql window-size
is a window option, so both machines size the same windows; the host's
setting is left as it is.
.Pp
A rename follows through.
.Ql prefix $
inside a mirror session goes to the host, where tmux renames the grouped
session to what was typed; the host's daemon takes that as the session's
new name, renames
.Ql api
to it and the grouped session to
.Ql web~ Ns Ar machine ,
and moves the session's project colour.
A session renamed on the host any other way, with
.Ic sessions rename
or tmux itself, has its grouped sessions renamed to match.
This machine finds the session again by its tmux id, which a rename keeps,
renames the mirror session
.Ql api@vm
to
.Ql web@vm
and its
.Ql @tc-remote
to
.Ql web ,
and says
.Ql api on vm is now web
on the message line; the mirror pane attaches to the new name when it next
connects.
A host from before this build does neither, and the mirror keeps the old
name until it is opened again.
.Pp
The mirror session sets
.Ql prefix
to
.Ql None :
in it, and only in it, the prefix key goes to the host's tmux, and this
machine's prefix is out of reach.
.Ql M-s
is this machine's switcher there as everywhere, and is the way out.
A
.Ql prefix2
you set stays this machine's.
.Ql M-a
and
.Ql M-A
go on to the host, whose own switcher moves through its windows:
.Ic switch windows
sees
.Ql @tc-host
on the session and sends the key to the mirror pane.
The session also carries
.Ql @tc-host
and
.Ql @tc-remote ,
naming the host and the session there, and
.Ql detach-on-destroy previous ,
so a detach on the host closes it and puts the client back where it came
from.
The message line says
.Ql api on vm
when the switch to it happens.
.Pp
The bar in a mirror session is this machine's.
The daemon asks the link for the host session's git, agents and health once
a second while the mirror session has a client, builds the right side from
those words itself, never from markup the host sends, and sets it as the
session's
.Ql @tc-right
in every bar mode, followed by this machine's own health as
.Ql here\&: Ar reason
when it has one.
With the link down the right side says
.Ql vm: no link .
The example configs read
.Ql @tc-right
first when
.Ql @tc-host
is set, and draw
.Ql @vm
and the ssh glyph on the left in the host colour, the tmux options
.Ql @tc-host-fg
and
.Ql @tc-host-bg ,
which the ssh hostname on the bar uses too.
The pickers draw each host's name in its own
.Ic colour
instead, so two hosts' rows read apart.
.Pp
The window list in a mirror session is the host's.
A mirror session has one window here, the pane the attach runs in, so the
host sends the grouped session's windows with its segments and the daemon
draws them in that window's entry: each as
.Ql index:name ,
with
.Ql *
on the current one and
.Ql \&!
on one that rang, the current one in the window's own
.Ql window-status-current-style
and the rest in its
.Ql window-status-style ,
joined by
.Ql window-status-separator .
It does so by setting the session option
.Ql @tc-windows
and pointing the window's
.Ql window-status-format
and
.Ql window-status-current-format
at it, with the window's two styles kept in
.Ql @tc-windows-style
and blanked meanwhile; with the link down, or a host whose build sends no
windows, the window gets its own entry and styles back.
A window name is text: a
.Ql #
in it is drawn, never expanded.
A click on one of the host's windows in the list selects the mirror
window and nothing more; the window key, or the host's prefix, moves
between them.
.Pp
The theme in a mirror session is the host session's.
.Ql prefix C-t
there goes to the host, as every prefix key does, and the pick paints the
host session and every session grouped with it; see
.Ic theme pick .
The host sends the theme with its segments, as its file name and the
.Ql @theme-*
settings that file makes, and the daemon here sets them on the mirror
session between this machine's own
.Pa _reset.tmux
and
.Pa _apply.tmux .
They are set as arguments, never sourced: a key is
.Ql @theme-
and lowercase letters, digits and
.Ql - ,
a value a tmux colour, or words for
.Ql @theme-name ,
and anything else is dropped.
A theme this machine has no file for is written to its themes directory, so
the picker here lists it; a file already there by that name is left alone.
A change of theme is said on the message line,
.Ql theme Amber on api@vm ;
opening a mirror session paints it without a word.
The last theme heard is kept while the link is down.
Before the first one arrives, and with a host whose build sends none, the
.Ql session-created
hook paints the mirror session as the session on the host,
.Ql api
rather than
.Ql api@vm ,
which with
.Ic [theme] default = Qq by-name
is the colour the host gives it by itself.
Nothing is painted without a
.Pa _apply.tmux
here; run
.Ic theme init
first.
.Pp
When the attach ends other than by a detach the pane says how in one line,
.Ql the connection to vm ended: Connection timed out ,
or
.Ql api no longer exists on vm ,
and waits:
.Ql r
connects again and
.Ql q
closes the mirror session.
Nothing is timed, and a link coming back does not reconnect on its own.
.Pp
The rest of this machine's surfaces read the hosts too.
.Ic brief
gives each host one line,
.Ql vm: 4 sessions, 1 agent asked, last heard 12s ,
or
.Ql vm: 4 sessions, last heard 3d, unreachable: Connection timed out ,
from what the daemon already holds; it starts no link, since it runs when a
client attaches.
The
.Ic project
picker lists every session on every host, as
.Ic switch sessions
does, and the directories of one place: this machine's source when it is
opened from a local session, and inside a mirror session that host's own
configured source, asked over the link and listed as
.Ql api@vm .
The host runs its own source; nothing this machine sends names a command.
Picking a host's directory opens a mirror session whose pane runs
.Ic project --detached Ar dir
on the host before the attach, and a path typed there, in the picker or in
.Ic switch sessions ,
is the host's to read.
.Pp
While a host's link is up its waiting agents are in this machine's
.Ic inbox
and
.Ic brief ,
each with
.Ql on vm
after its place, and an agent that asks there plays this machine's earcon,
held by quiet hours here like any other.
Their words are text: control characters go and nothing in them reaches a
tmux format.
Picking one opens the mirror session with the agent's window and pane chosen
on the host after the attach; with the mirror session already open, its
pane's attach is started again with the same choice, one reconnect.
The link itself stays read-only.
.Pp
Snapshots keep a mirror session with its host and the session there, its
pane recorded as
.Ql tmux-companion host attach vm api .
.Ic sessions resurrect
reopens it through a shipped
.Ic [[restore.program]]
row that adds
.Fl -wait :
the pane says
.Ql vm: press r to connect
and runs no ssh until
.Ql r ,
so a restore after a reboot doesn't start a dozen handshakes at once.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic hosts Op Fl -print
In a terminal, a picker of the hosts: each host's line, then its sessions,
a session whose mirror is open here with
.Ql open here
after it.
Each row's preview ends with the keys it takes, one a line.
On a host's line, Enter brings its link up now, as
.Ic hosts connect
does; ctrl-e opens the add form filled in to edit it, as
.Ic hosts edit
does; ctrl-x asks, keep first, and forgets it; ctrl-o makes a session on
it, asking for the name and the directory; ctrl-w closes every mirror of it
open here.
On a session, Enter opens its mirror here and switches to it; ctrl-w closes
that mirror and leaves the session on the host, as
.Ic host close
does; ctrl-x asks what
.Ic switch sessions
asks, keep, kill it on the host or forget the host; ctrl-r asks for a new
name and renames it on the host, as
.Ic host rename
does.
ctrl-n adds a host from any row.
What a key did is said in the footer in words, and the list opens again on
the same row.
With no hosts, it is the add form.
.Pp
With
.Fl -print ,
or off a terminal, each host on one line, in the order the configuration
lists them and then
the ones added for this tmux server: its name, its link mode, whether the
link is up and why not when it isn't, when it is up whether the host is
.Ql pushing
or
.Ql polled every 5s
(its
.Ic poll_secs ,
for a bridge that doesn't serve
.Ql watch ) ,
when the host was last heard from, how
many sessions it has, its build and its tmux, which methods its bridge lacks
when it is older than this one, and whether the bridge had to start the
host's daemon.
Under each host, one line per session in the host's own order of use, as
.Ql api@vm, 3 windows ,
with
.Ql attached on vm
when somebody there is in it.
Sessions on the host that are another machine's view of one of its own,
carrying
.Ql @tc-mirror ,
are never listed.
It reads what the daemon has and starts no link.
.It Ic hosts add Oo Ar name Oo Fl -save Oc Fl - Ar command ... Oc
Add a host.
The name is letters, digits,
.Ql -
and
.Ql _ ,
up to 32, since it ends up in session names, file names and shell lines.
The command is an argv, or one argument split by shell-word rules without a
shell;
.Ql $ ,
.Ql | ,
.Ql \&;
and backticks are refused either way.
Without
.Fl -save
the host is kept in the tmux server option
.Ql @tc-hosts
until the server exits; with it, a
.Ic [[hosts]]
entry is added to
.Pa config.toml ,
keeping every comment in the file.
A name already in use is refused with the command that host is reached with.
.Pp
With no arguments, in a terminal, it is a form: two labelled lines, name and
command, Tab between them, the cursor in the one being typed into.
The name fills itself in from the command until you type your own: the
command's last word that doesn't start with
.Ql - ,
any
.Ql user@
dropped, cut at the first
.Ql \&. ,
so
.Ql ssh dev@vm.local
gives
.Ql vm ,
and
.Ql vm-2
when
.Ql vm
is taken.
Enter tests the command with one hello and says
.Ql vm answered: tmux-companion 0.10.0, tmux 3.4
or why not; then
.Ql s
keeps the host in
.Pa config.toml ,
.Ql t
keeps it for this tmux server, any other key goes back to editing, and Esc
leaves.
Each state is one line.
It has no key of its own;
.Ic setup
offers one, and in
.Ic switch sessions
ctrl-n on a host's row opens it.
.It Ic hosts forget Ar name
Forget a host: its cached sessions, and its
.Ic [[hosts]]
entry in
.Pa config.toml ,
every comment kept, or its place in
.Ql @tc-hosts .
Refused, with the reason, while a mirror session to it is open.
.It Ic hosts edit Ar name Oo Fl -rename Ar new Oc Op Fl - Ar command ...
Change a host where it is kept: its
.Ic [[hosts]]
entry in
.Pa config.toml
edited in place, its other keys, its place and every comment kept, or its
place in
.Ql @tc-hosts .
The command after
.Fl -
replaces its command, read as
.Ic hosts add
reads one; without one it keeps the command it has.
.Fl -rename
gives it a new name, refused while a mirror session to it is open and when
the name is another host's.
One line says what changed:
.Ql vm is now box, reached with ssh box .
In the
.Ic hosts
picker ctrl-e opens the add form filled in, whose
.Ql s
keeps the change.
.It Xo Ic host attach Ar host Ar session
.Op Fl -create Op Fl -in Ar dir
.Op Fl -dir Ar dir
.Op Fl -window Ar index
.Op Fl -pane Ar id
.Op Fl -wait
.Xc
What a mirror session's pane runs: one line,
.Ql connecting to vm for api ,
then the host command with
.Fl t
and the grouped attach, in this terminal, so anything ssh asks is asked
where you can see it.
.Fl -create
makes the session on the host first when it isn't there, in
.Fl -in Ar dir
when given, with
.Ql ~
the host's home; a
.Ar dir
the host doesn't have makes nothing, and the pane says
.Ql docs wasn't made on vm: the directory asked for isn't there .
.Fl -dir
runs
.Ic project --detached Ar dir
on the host first and attaches to the session it names.
.Fl -window
and
.Fl -pane
choose that window and that pane, a
.Ql \&%N
id, on the host once attached.
.Fl -wait
says
.Ql vm: press r to connect
and waits for
.Ql r
before the first connect, or
.Ql q
to close.
Every name goes into the remote shell line quoted, so a session called
.Ql x'; touch pwned; '
is a name and nothing else.
.It Ic host kill Ar host Ar session
Kill
.Ar session
on
.Ar host
through the host command, with every session in its group there, the
.Ql api~mac
sessions other machines' mirrors attach through among them, and close its
mirror session here when one is open.
One line says how it went:
.Ql killed api on vm ,
.Ql api was already gone from vm ,
or
.Ql could not kill api on vm:
and the reason.
.It Ic host rename Ar host Ar session Ar new
Rename
.Ar session
on
.Ar host
to
.Ar new
through the host command, with plain tmux, so the host's build doesn't
matter.
The host's daemon moves the grouped sessions mirrors attach through, and a
mirror session open here follows once the host's list says the new name.
One line:
.Ql api on vm is now web ,
or why not:
.Ql vm already has a session called web ,
.Ql api is no longer on vm .
.It Ic host close Ar host Ar session
Close the mirror session of
.Ar session
on
.Ar host
here.
The session stays on the host, and so does every other machine's mirror of
it.
One line:
.Ql closed api@vm here; api stays on vm ,
or exit 1 with
.Ql api@vm isn't open here .
.It Ic hosts connect Op Ar name
Start or retry a host's link now, or every host's, and say how it went on
one line each:
.Ql vm answered: tmux-companion 0.10.0, tmux 3.4 ,
or the reason it didn't, such as
.Ql vm needs a login: run ssh vm once in a terminal .
An on-demand link stays up for
.Ic idle_secs
after the last ask.
Exits 1 when any host didn't answer.
.It Ic bridge
Serve the link on stdin and stdout, answering from this machine's daemon and
starting it when none runs.
This is what a host command runs on the host, not something to type.
.El
.Pp
Configuration, one table per host:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
[[hosts]]
name = "vm"
command = ["ssh", "vm"]
link = "on-demand"
poll_secs = 5
idle_secs = 600
connect_secs = 10
ping_secs = 15
open = "mirror"
colour = ""
.Ed
.Pp
.Ic colour
is the colour the host's name is drawn in on every picker row,
.Ql colourN
or
.Ql #rrggbb .
Empty, the default, takes the next of eight colours in host order, starting
at
.Ql colour203 ,
so two hosts never share one until there are more than eight.
The name is always in the words as well, as
.Ql @vm ,
and every row of a host that can't be reached is dimmed and says
.Ql unreachable .
.Pp
.Ic link
is when the link is up.
.Ql on-demand ,
the default, starts none until something asks for the host and closes it
.Ic idle_secs
after the last ask.
.Ql while-attached
keeps it up while any client is attached to the local tmux server: it is the
mode for a host where agents run long, because an on-demand link hears a
host's agents only while something holds the link up, so a question asked
there while you work here waits until you look.
.Ql off
never starts one; the host is still listed.
.Ic connect_secs
is how long the host has to say hello, or to answer a question, before the
link is called down.
.Ic ping_secs
is how often a host that pushes is pinged, and
.Ic poll_secs
how often one that can't is asked.
.Ic open
is what picking one of the host's sessions does, in
.Ic switch sessions ,
the project picker and
.Ic sessions start :
.Ql mirror ,
the default, opens a mirror session;
.Ql handover
hands this terminal to the host.
tmux replaces this terminal's client with the host command,
.Ql ssh vm -t 'tmux attach-session -t =api' ,
after saying
.Ql api on vm, handed over: detach there to come back to here
on the message line, and the terminal says
.Ql connecting to vm for api .
The host's tmux then owns the terminal: its bar, its prefix and its keys,
with one bar on the screen and it the host's.
This machine's bar,
.Ic switch sessions
and the rest of its keys are out of reach until you come back, though the
inbox's sounds still play.
A detach on the host comes back with
.Ql exec tmux attach-session -t =here
on this server, to the session the client left; a connection that ends any
other way says so in one line and waits for Enter before coming back.
Outside tmux the attach runs in the terminal it was typed in, with nothing to
come back to.
.Ic sessions start Fl -detach
always opens a mirror session, since a handover has to have a client to hand
over, and so do an inbox pick and a host's directory, which chain a pane or a
project onto the attach that only the mirror's attach carries.
.Sh COPY MODE AND OPENING
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic open Oo Ar text Oc Op Fl s , Fl i , Fl n , Fl d Ar dir , Fl -pane Ar id , Fl -cursor-x Ar column
Open what is in the text: a URL goes to a browser, a
.Ql path:line:column
opens an editor there.
.Fl -cursor-x
names the column the cursor is in, and what sits under it wins.
The shipped binding passes the line under the cursor together with
.Ql #{copy_cursor_x} ,
so nothing has to be selected first: being on the thing is the whole gesture,
and a line naming two paths opens the one the cursor is actually on rather than
whichever came first.
.Fl s
reads the tmux selection instead of arguments, for a binding that would rather
select a range by hand.
.Fl i ,
or
.Fl -choose ,
asks which application opens it, from the
.Ic [[open.application]]
list.
With nothing in that list there is nothing to choose between, so it opens what
it would have opened anyway rather than showing an empty picker.
.Fl n
prints what it would open and opens nothing.
.Fl -pane
names the pane this is for, and the shipped binding passes
.Ql #{pane_id}
so that it does.
tmux runs the binding through
.Ic run-shell ,
where
.Ev TMUX_PANE
holds the most recently active pane on the server rather than the pane the key
was pressed in, so without it the editor can open in a window nobody is looking
at.
Bound to
.Ql o
in copy mode.
.It Ic clipboard
Copy standard input to the system clipboard, whichever command this platform
has.
Bound to
.Ql y
in copy mode.
.It Ic shell-init Ar shell
Print the shell code that emits the OSC 133 prompt marks.
tmux has had
.Ic next-prompt
and
.Ic previous-prompt
since 3.3 and they do nothing until the shell says where a prompt begins.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
eval "$(tmux-companion shell-init zsh)"     # or bash
tmux-companion shell-init fish | source
.Ed
On its first prompt the shell sets the pane option
.Ql @tmux-companion-marks
to 1, once, inside tmux only.
.El
.Sh THEMES
A theme is a file of tmux options that
.Pa _apply.tmux
turns into colours.
Options are set without
.Fl g ,
so a theme belongs to one session and two projects can be different colours
at once.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic theme init Op Fl -themes Ar dir
Write the starter themes and the two files that apply them.
Six colours, which
.Ic theme gen Fl -shades
grows to 151.
Nothing is overwritten.
.It Ic theme gen Oo Fl -apply Oc Oo Fl -shades Oc Op Fl -background Ar colour
Compute each theme's readable text colour and a visible border.
Without
.Fl -apply
it reports what would change and touches nothing.
.Fl -shades Op Ar level
writes a theme for every colour in the 6x6x6 cube whose text clears a contrast
floor, named after the bundled colour each sits nearest to, so
.Ql ember-04
and
.Ql pine-11
land together in the picker.
.Ar level
is a rung of a ladder, and the flag on its own means
.Ql aaa :
.Bl -column "aaa " "12:1 " "themes" -compact -offset indent
.It Ic aa Ta 4.5:1 Ta 216
.It Ic aaa Ta 7:1 Ta 151
.It Ic a4 Ta 9.5:1 Ta 105
.It Ic a5 Ta 12:1 Ta 75
.It Ic a6 Ta \(em Ta 18
.El
.Pp
WCAG names the first two rungs and stops; the rest continue at its own spacing
of 2.5 per step, so the ladder is one rule rather than four opinions.
.Ic a6
is not a floor at all: it is the bundled six with a lighter and a darker
sibling of each, which is what
.Fl -shades
did before it swept the cube, and the only rung whose colours a person chose.
.Ic aaa
is the default rather than
.Ic aa
because the worst colour in the cube scores 4.60:1, so an AA filter keeps all
216 of them
.Pq a threshold that is arithmetically a no-op .
The text colour is chosen to clear 4.5:1 against the theme's own background
and the border to clear 3:1 against the terminal's, so a theme that cannot be
read cannot be written.
.It Ic theme add Fl -bg Ar colour Op Fl -fg Ar colour
A theme from one colour.
The text colour is computed unless given, and a given pair under 4.5:1 is
refused without
.Fl -force .
.It Ic theme apply Oo Ar session Oc Op Fl t Ar target , Fl -all
The theme a session should have, without asking.
Run from the
.Ic session-created
hook.
Silent when there is no theme to apply, which is every install until
.Ic theme init
has run.
With
.Ic [theme]
.Ql default = "by-name"
a session nothing claims gets one of the six bundled themes chosen from its
name, the same one every time, so every session has a colour of its own with
nothing picked.
.Fl -all
repaints every session instead of one, which is what a reload needs: sourcing
.Pa tmux.conf
resets the global options a theme sets, so without it every session is left
wearing whatever the file says rather than its own colour.
.It Ic theme list-colours Op Fl -print
Every colour tmux takes, painted, with its hex and the contrast on it.
.Fl -print
is one name per line with no swatch, for piping somewhere;
.Fl -plain
is the old spelling and still works.
.El
.Sh SOUNDS
A short sound for something a sighted person would see on the bar, for
somebody who hears the screen rather than glancing at it.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic earcon Ar event
Play the sound
.Ar event
makes,
.Ql asked ,
.Ql done ,
.Ql health
or
.Ql chunk ,
now, whether sounds are on or not, and say which command it ran.
With
.Ic [earcons] enabled ,
the daemon plays them itself: once per pass when agents start waiting on you
or finish, and when a health reason appears that was not there before, checked
every ten seconds whether or not a bar draws the health mark.
.Ic [earcons] on
says which events sound, and
.Ic asked ,
.Ic done ,
.Ic health
and
.Ic chunk
the command each runs, the platform's own sound when empty.
Quiet hours hold them; a health reason that appeared during them sounds when
they end.
.El
.Sh CONFIGURATION
TOML, read from the first of:
.Bl -bullet -offset indent -compact
.It
.Ev TMUX_COMPANION_CONFIG
.It
.Pa $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/tmux-companion/config.toml
.It
.Pa ~/.config/tmux-companion/config.toml
.It
.Pa ~/.tmux-companion.toml
.El
.Pp
Every setting with its default, annotated, is
.Pa docs/config.example.toml .
The sections are
.Ic [general] , [dirs] , [git] , [network] , [battery] , [glyphs] ,
.Ic [status] , [sh_jobs] , [usage] , [[layout]] , [autoreload] ,
.Ic [window_names] , [notify] , [agents] , [project] , [autosave] ,
.Ic [sessions] , [[restore.program]] , [run] , [clipboard] , [theme] , [bar] ,
.Ic [picker] , [journal] , [online] , [open] , [earcons] , [chunk] ,
.Ic [keys] , [switcher]
and
.Ic [[hosts]] .
A
.Ic [toggle]
section, from a command that has gone, is ignored, and
.Ic config check
says so.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic config path
Which file is being read.
.It Ic config check
Parse it and say what is wrong, with the line and a suggestion for a
misspelled key or an unrecognised value.
It also names any setting that still works but is deprecated.
.It Ic config dump
Every setting with its default, as a config file.
.It Ic config init Op Fl -force
Write a short starter config where
.Ic config path
would read it, creating the directory, and print the path.
The file sets the glyph preset, the directory source and the snapshot timer,
and carries the editor-beside-an-agent layout as a comment to uncomment; the
rest is left to the defaults, which
.Ic config dump
prints in full.
A file already there is refused unless
.Fl -force .
.El
.Pp
Two settings are worth knowing before the rest.
.Ic [bar] background
is what the segments draw their caps against and has to match
.Ev status-style ;
it used to be a compiled-in
.Ql colour233 ,
which is one person's bar and nobody else's.
.Ic [bar] mode
is
.Ql call ,
tmux starting a client from
.Ql #()
every
.Ic status-interval ;
.Ql push ,
the daemon setting the session option
.Ql @tc-right
once a second for
.Ic status-right
to expand without starting anything, and the same option on each pane whose
directory is not its session's, so a window or pane switch shows the new
place at once; or
.Ql auto ,
the default, which pushes when the global
.Ic status-right
reads
.Ql @tc-right
and tmux is 3.4 or newer.
.Ic doctor
says which it chose and why.
After a window or pane switch the bar follows in tens of milliseconds in
either mode, and push is the sooner of the two: a median of 16 ms against 40
for call on one idle Intel MacBook.
.Ic [picker] preview
is where every picker puts its preview pane, or
.Ql none
for no preview at all.
The rest of that section is what a picker looks like rather than what it holds:
.Ic border ,
.Ic label_position ,
.Ic hint_position ,
which the brief, the restore screen, the run dialog and the cheat sheet follow
too, so
.Ql hidden
takes the line of keys off every popup while a restore counting down still
says when it goes ahead,
.Ic hint_across ,
which makes that line a row across the whole popup over the list and the
preview both,
.Ic prompt_position ,
.Ic list_from ,
.Ic counter ,
.Ic rules ,
.Ic marker ,
.Ic column_order
and the preview's own label.
Each of them can be written again under
.Ic [picker.keys] , [picker.project] , [picker.window] , [picker.theme] ,
.Ic [picker.run] , [picker.open] , [picker.panes] , [picker.setup]
or
.Ic [picker.switch]
for the one picker that wants a different answer, together with the
.Ic label , hint
and
.Ic preview_label
that picker says.
How big the popup itself is belongs to
.Ic display-popup
in
.Pa tmux.conf ,
not here.
.Ic [agents] programs
is the one list of what counts as a coding agent, matched against the name
each pane's program was started as.
.Ql pane_current_command
is matched as it is unless it can hide the program: a bare version
.Pq claude, Ql 2.1.296 ,
a release after a hyphen
.Pq grok, Ql grok-1.0.50-mac ,
an interpreter
.Po gemini and cursor-agent are
.Ql node ,
aider
.Ql python3
.Pc
or a name 15 bytes long.
For those the program in front is named from its arguments:
.Ql argv[0]
for a native program, the script for an interpreter, so
.Ql node /usr/local/bin/gemini
is gemini.
This reads the process directly and starts none.
When it cannot be read, a bare version still counts as claude and a name with
a release version after a hyphen as the name before it.
The list is read by
.Ic panes Fl -agents ,
the
.Ic agents
segment and the restore headline alike;
.Ic waiting_secs
is how long one has to be quiet before it counts as waiting on you, when it
has not said better through
.Ic agent ,
.Ic interval_secs
how often the bar's segment re-reads the pane list, and
.Ic show
whether the count after the total is the agents waiting, the agents busy, or
both.
.Ic [open] split
is which side the editor opens on,
.Ql right
or
.Ql bottom .
.Pp
.Ic [project] trusted
is the paths under which a checkout's
.Pa .tmux-companion.toml
may run what it says; see
.Sx FILES .
.Ic [project] dirs_source
is where the project picker gets its directories, one of
.Ql zoxide ,
.Ql z ,
.Ql cdr ,
.Ql ghq
or
.Ql none .
.Xr zoxide 1
is the default rather than a requirement, and nothing here has to be installed:
a source that is absent lists nothing, leaving live sessions and whatever is
typed.
.Ql z
reads the
.Pa ~/.z
database and
.Ql cdr
reads
.Pa ~/.chpwd-recent-dirs ,
as files rather than as commands, because both are shell functions that cannot
be run from here.
.Ic [project] dirs_command
takes any command printing one absolute path per line, as a list of words, and
wins over
.Ic dirs_source ;
.Ic [project] visit_command
is what records a pick, defaulting to
.Ql zoxide add
for zoxide and nothing for the rest.
.Ic [project] zoxide
is the old spelling of
.Ql dirs_source = \(dqnone\(dq
and goes away in the next release.
.Pp
.Ic [[git.branch_types]]
is the glyph drawn before a branch name, keyed on how the name starts.
Each entry is an
.Ic icon
and a list of
.Ic prefixes ,
tried in the order written; the first prefix that claims the name wins, and
it is cut off the name the bar draws, so
.Ql feat/status-bar
renders as the feature glyph and
.Ql status-bar .
Prefixes are compared without regard to case and are plain text rather than
regular expressions.
.Ic icon
takes
.Ql {NAME}
for any glyph the tool knows, so the file stays readable without a patched
font, and draws anything else as written.
Twenty-five groups ship, from
.Ql feat/
and
.Ql fix/
to
.Ql docs/ ,
.Ql perf/ ,
.Ql parked/
and
.Ql a11y/ ;
.Ic config dump
prints them in the shape they are edited in.
The list adds to them rather than replacing them: its entries are tried
first, then every shipped entry it does not repeat, so an entry wins for the
prefixes it names and every other prefix keeps its glyph.
An empty
.Ic icon
claims its prefixes for the plain branch glyph and keeps the name whole, which
is how a shipped entry is turned off.
A name with no
.Ql word/
prefix keeps the plain branch glyph, which is why
.Ql main
and
.Ql master
need no entry.
.Pp
A
.Ql word/
prefix nothing in the list claims is looked up among the Nerd Font Material
Design glyph names built into the binary: the word as written, then as a
singular, then from
.Ql -ed
or
.Ql -ing ,
then as a plural, skipping any glyph the bar already draws for something
else, so
.Ql cleanup/
finds the broom.
The daemon keeps each pick in
.Pa $XDG_STATE_HOME/tmux-companion/branch-icons.toml
so the same prefix draws the same glyph after a restart or an upgrade;
.Ic doctor
lists the picks with the
.Ic icon
value that makes one an entry of your own.
A prefix the lookup cannot place draws a catch-all glyph with the name whole.
.Ic [git] branch_icon_matching = false
turns the lookup off, and so does the ascii glyph preset, since a looked-up
glyph has no 7-bit form.
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic doctor
What a bug report needs: the version, the socket, what the config says, the
last line of the daemon log, whether either autosave is running and when it
last did, every reason the health mark would show, what tmux says, and what is
missing.
A daemon from another build, one that does not answer, and a config file
edited after the daemon started each end their line with
.Ql run tmux-companion restart ,
because that is the answer to all three.
The report ends on tmux's own options, the ones that cost something as they
are set: an
.Ql escape-time
over 50 ms, a
.Ql history-limit
of 2000 lines or under, a
.Ql display-time
under two seconds, a
.Ql status-interval
over five or of zero,
.Ql focus-events ,
.Ql set-clipboard ,
.Ql monitor-bell
or
.Ql mouse
turned off, a
.Ql default-terminal
with no 256 colours in its name, and, with hosts configured, a
.Ql set-clipboard
other than
.Ql on ,
which keeps a copy made in a mirror session on the host.
Each gets the value it has, what that costs, and the line for
.Pa tmux.conf
under it.
Nothing is set: the file is yours, and
.Ql mouse
for one is a preference.
A tmux with nothing to change says so in one line.
Most people got these settings from
.Lk https://github.com/tmux-plugins/tmux-sensible tmux-sensible ,
which set them as a plugin and hasn't been pushed since April 2024.
The
.Ql setup
line counts the items
.Ic setup
lists as open, and the
.Ql hosts
line says what
.Ic hosts
says about each host, from a daemon already running.
.It Ic health Op Cm ack
Why the health mark is up, one reason a line, or
.Ql ok .
A timer or a segment that failed stays a reason for an hour, until the same
timer runs and works, or until
.Ic health ack
forgets it, whichever comes first;
.Cm ack
prints each failure it forgot.
It forgets failures and unclean stops and nothing else.
A
.Pa config.toml
edited after the daemon started, a newer binary on disk, quiet hours and a
network that's gone are how things are, so they stay, and
.Cm ack
lists them under what it forgot as
.Ql still on the mark .
The sessions timer finding no tmux server, or a server with no session in it,
isn't a failure and is never a reason: every session closed is something
people do, and the daemon is still there when tmux comes back.
.It Xo Ic health note Ar source Ar text ...
.Op Fl -ttl Ar duration
.Xc
.It Ic health note Ar source Fl -clear
Put a reason on the mark from outside the daemon: a dotfiles check, a build,
anything that wants the bar to say something until it is fixed.
It is said as
.Ql source: text ,
or as the text alone when it already starts with
.Ql source: ,
after the daemon's own reasons, in
.Ic health ,
.Ic brief
and behind the bar's health mark.
.Ar source
is letters, digits,
.Ql - ,
.Ql _
and
.Ql \&. ,
up to 32, and each source has one note: saying it again replaces it in
place, so a check that runs daily keeps one line.
The words of
.Ar text
are joined with spaces into one line of up to 120 characters.
.Fl -ttl
takes it away after
.Ar duration ,
.Ql 90m ,
.Ql 12h
or
.Ql 2d ,
unless it is said again first, for a source that might stop running;
without it a note lasts until
.Fl -clear .
.Ic health ack
leaves notes standing, since their source says when they stop being true.
Notes are kept in
.Pa $XDG_STATE_HOME/tmux-companion/health-notes.json
and read back when the daemon starts.
On another machine's mirror bar a host's note is part of that host's own
health segment.
One line says what happened:
.Ql noted dotfiles: 3 drifted ,
.Ql cleared dotfiles's note
or
.Ql dotfiles had no note .
.It Ic probe
What the terminal answers about itself.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "$XDG_STATE_HOME/tmux-companion/projects/" -compact
.It Pa ~/.config/tmux-companion/config.toml
the configuration
.It Pa .tmux-companion.toml
in a project root, the layout that checkout opens with:
.Ic [[window]]
rows in the shape of
.Ic [[layout.window]] ,
or
.Ic layout
naming one of the reader's own; its commands run only under a path
.Ic [project] trusted
lists, and elsewhere the names alone are taken.
.Ic config check
run inside the checkout reads it too
.It Pa ~/.config/tmux/themes/
themes, and the two files that apply them
.It Pa ~/Library/DoNotDisturb/DB/
macOS's Focus database, read and never written, with
.Ic [quiet] follow_focus
.It Pa $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/dconf/user
GNOME's settings database, read and never written, on Linux with
.Ic [quiet] follow_focus
.It Pa $XDG_STATE_HOME/tmux-companion/projects/
saved per-project layouts
.It Pa $XDG_STATE_HOME/tmux-companion/hosts/
each host's last answer,
.Pa <name>.bin ,
kept so a host is listed before its link is up
.It Pa $XDG_STATE_HOME/tmux-companion/health-notes.json
the reasons
.Ic health note
put on the mark, one per source, kept across a daemon restart
.It Pa $XDG_STATE_HOME/tmux-companion/branch-icons.toml
the glyph the daemon picked for each branch prefix it looked up by glyph
name; delete it and restart the daemon to pick again
.It Pa $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/tmux-companion/keys.toml
the
.Ic [keys]
settings and tables, without the
.Ql keys.
prefix, beside the config file; both files count, and a name set in both is
an error
.It Pa $XDG_STATE_HOME/tmux-companion/keys-discovered.json
what
.Ic keys discover
found in each layer, read by
.Ic keys collide
.It Pa $XDG_STATE_HOME/tmux-companion/keys-route.tmux
the script the last
.Ic keys route
sourced, kept for reading when a wrapper misbehaves
.It Pa $XDG_STATE_HOME/tmux-companion/crashes.tsv
one line for every time the daemon started and found that the one before it
had stopped without cleaning up, a crash or a
.Ql kill -9 :
the unix time it noticed, and the last heartbeat the old one wrote.
Two lines within a minute put a second reason on the health mark
.It Pa $XDG_STATE_HOME/tmux-companion/keys-usage.tsv
which bindings the key picker ran, written only with
.Ic [usage] enabled
on: one
.Ar table key @secs
line per pick, the time in unix seconds, until it passes five thousand lines,
then one
.Ar table key count first last
line per binding under a
.Ql #v2
header.
A log written before picks had a time, raw or under
.Ql #v1 ,
is still read
.It Pa $XDG_STATE_HOME/tmux-companion/VERSION
the build that last started a daemon here, rewritten on every start
.It Pa $XDG_STATE_HOME/tmux-companion/sessions/running
the pid of the running daemon, written at start and removed by a clean stop,
which includes
.Dv SIGTERM
and
.Dv SIGINT
.It Pa $XDG_STATE_HOME/tmux-companion/sessions/crashed
the
.Pa running
file of a daemon that never removed it, moved here by the next daemon to
start once it has found that pid dead; its presence is what makes
.Ic sessions resurrect
treat the last snapshot as taken before a crash
.It Pa $XDG_STATE_HOME/tmux-companion/setup-skipped.tsv
the ids
.Ic setup
was told to skip, one per line
.It Pa $XDG_STATE_HOME/tmux-companion/build-seen
the build a daemon first ran here and when, tab-separated
.It Pa $XDG_STATE_HOME/tmux-companion/journal.tsv
what ran long, what the agents asked, what opened and closed
.It Pa $XDG_STATE_HOME/tmux-companion/daemon.log
the daemon's stderr: one line per start, one per failure, unless
.Ic [general] log
points elsewhere
.It Pa /tmp/tmux-companion-$UID.sock
the daemon's socket
.It Pa /tmp/tmux-companion-$UID.sock.lock
the lock one daemon holds for the life of the process, so a second start
cannot unlink the socket out from under the first
.It Pa ~/.z
the z database, read when
.Ic [project] dirs_source
is
.Ql z
.It Pa ~/.chpwd-recent-dirs
zsh's recent directories, read when
.Ic [project] dirs_source
is
.Ql cdr
.El
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width TMUX_COMPANION_TMUX_CONF -compact
.It Ev TMUX_COMPANION_CONFIG
a configuration file, before the search order
.It Ev TMUX_COMPANION_SOCK
the daemon socket, before the default.
A client replaces a daemon from an older build and leaves a newer one alone,
saying so once, so a development build run beside the installed one wants a
socket of its own here rather than the two taking turns replacing each other's
daemon
.It Ev TMUX_COMPANION_TRACE
a file each process appends timing marks to, one line per mark: its pid, what
happened
.Pq Li main , chrome , rows , paint , drawn , key , tmux
and the wall-clock time.
.Pa scripts/bench-capped.sh
reads it to split a picker's opening into phases and to count the tmux
processes the daemon starts; unset, nothing is written
.It Ev TMUX_COMPANION_TMUX_CONF
the tmux configuration the daemon watches for key bindings and
.Ic [autoreload] ,
before the first of
.Pa $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/tmux/tmux.conf
and
.Pa ~/.tmux.conf
that exists
.It Ev XDG_CONFIG_HOME , XDG_STATE_HOME
where configuration and state live
.It Ev _Z_DATA
the z database, before
.Pa ~/.z
.It Ev ZDOTDIR
where zsh keeps
.Pa .chpwd-recent-dirs ,
before the home directory
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
.Pp
A binding that runs from
.Ic run-shell
prints its complaint on standard output rather than exiting non-zero, because
tmux turns a non-zero exit into
.Ql 'tmux-companion open -s' returned 1
in the message area, which names the command and not the problem.
